From: Veron
Subject: [PATCH] stdlib: free(NULL) is a no-op

M2libc's free walks the allocated list looking for the block, and if it does
not find one it reaches

    /* we received a pointer to a block that was not allocated */
    /* Bail *HARD* because I do not want to cover this edge case */
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);

free(NULL) takes exactly that path. C requires free(NULL) to do nothing, and
real code depends on it -- tcc's tcc_set_str frees the old string before
storing a new one, and the old string is NULL the first time:

    static void tcc_set_str(char **pp, const char *str)
    { tcc_free(*pp); *pp = str ? tcc_strdup(str) : NULL; }

The symptom is a silent exit(1) with nothing on stderr, which is very hard to
trace back to free. Found by putting progress markers through tcc_new: the
last one printed was immediately before tcc_set_lib_path.

--- a/stdlib.c
+++ b/stdlib.c
@@ -134,6 +134,18 @@
 {
 /* just in case someone needs to quickly turn it off */
 #ifndef _MALLOC_DISABLE_FREE
+	/* free(NULL) IS A NO-OP. C requires it, and real code relies on it:
+	 *
+	 *     static void tcc_set_str(char **pp, const char *str)
+	 *     { tcc_free(*pp); *pp = str ? tcc_strdup(str) : NULL; }
+	 *
+	 * *pp is NULL the first time, straight out of a calloc'd struct. Without
+	 * this, the search below finds no matching block and falls through to the
+	 * exit(EXIT_FAILURE) at the end of the function -- so the program dies
+	 * with status 1 and NO message, which is a hard thing to trace back here.
+	 */
+	if(NULL == ptr) return;
+
 	struct _malloc_node* i = _allocated_list;
 	struct _malloc_node* last = NULL;
 
